        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Haitian Vodou</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 1512 CE – present</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Origin</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a>, in the French colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a> that became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a>, between the 16th and 19th centuries, among enslaved West and Central Africans, especially Fon and Bakongo; it took the form of Vodou around the mid-18th century.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Founder or early teachers</span><span class="info-stat-val">none: it arose from the blending of several West and Central African traditional religions with Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> and Freemasonry, and has no central authority or single leader.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Scriptures</span><span class="info-stat-val">none written: its teachings pass on orally and through ritual, song and the langaj of the rites, though many practitioners have used texts since these appeared in the mid-20th century.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Followers today</span><span class="info-stat-val">Never counted, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a> has had no accurate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>; in 1992 Leslie Desmangles put the Haitian practitioners at six million. Most Haitians practise both Vodou and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>, and communities live in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> and elsewhere.</span></div>
        </div>
