    <section class="kid-tale" aria-labelledby="kid-tale-title">
      <h2 class="kid-tale-title" id="kid-tale-title">The story of Haitian Vodou</h2>
      <ol class="kid-tale-pages"><li class="kid-tale-np"><p>More than 500 years ago, Spanish colonists began bringing enslaved people from West Africa to the island of Hispaniola.</p><p>The word Vodou comes from the Fon language of West Africa, where it meant a spirit.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/code-noir.jpg" alt="Code Noir" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Code Noir <span class="kid-tale-cr">France, Louis XV, Prault pere, Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>A French law forbade them to practise African religions openly, so they held their rites in secret, usually at night.</p><p>More than 250 years ago, their African religions mixed with Catholic ones and became Vodou.</p></li><li class="kid-tale-np"><p>Vodouists believe in one God, Bondye, who is so far away that they rarely pray to him directly.</p><p>Instead they serve the lwa, spirits who are often linked to Catholic saints.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/haitian-vodou.jpg" alt="Haitian Vodou" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Haitian Vodou <span class="kid-tale-cr">Sam Fentress, CC BY-SA 2.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>At night ceremonies of drums, songs and dancing, believers invite a lwa to come and speak through a worshipper.</p><p>Designs called vèvè are drawn on the temple floor in cornmeal to call the lwa.</p></li><li class="kid-tale-np"><p>Vodou became Haiti’s main faith after the Haitian Revolution, though outsiders long mocked it as sorcery.</p><p>Today most Haitians practise both Vodou and Catholicism, and Haitians have carried it to many other countries.</p></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Vodouists serve Bondye, a remote creator God, through the lwa, spirits mostly named after West and Central African deities, equated with Catholic saints and grouped in nations such as the Rada and the Petwo.</li><li>In the dans, the night ceremony of drumming, song and dance, a lwa is invited to possess a worshipper, the chwal or “horse”, and to speak through them to the congregation.</li><li>Forbidden in the colony, Vodou became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a>’s dominant religion after the Revolution; long maligned by outsiders as sorcery, it was officially recognised in 2003 and has spread with Haitian emigrants.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The word Vodou comes from the Fon language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>: Vôdoun, a word for a spirit or deity in the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-dahomey">Dahomey</a>, was first recorded in the Doctrina Christiana of 1658.</p></aside>
    </section>

