    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1512</span><span class="rf-text">Spanish colonists on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a> begin importing enslaved West Africans, some of them priests of African traditional religions</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1685</span><span class="rf-text">The French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/code-noir">Code Noir</a> forbids the open practice of African religions and orders that the enslaved be baptised as Roman Catholics</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1750</span><span class="rf-text">Among the enslaved of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a>, African religions mixed with Catholic and Freemasonic elements take the form of Vodou</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1791</span><span class="rf-text">According to legend, a Vodou ritual at Bois-Caïman sparks the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haitian-revolution">Haitian Revolution</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1805</span><span class="rf-text">The Roman Catholic Church cuts ties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a> until 1860, and Vodou becomes the country’s dominant religion</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1915</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> occupies <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a> until 1934, stirring foreign interest in Vodou and its reassessment by Haitian indigenists</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1941</span><span class="rf-text">The government launches Operation Nettoyage, a Church-backed campaign to destroy many Vodou temples</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">2003</span><span class="rf-text">President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-bertrand-aristide">Jean-Bertrand Aristide</a> grants Vodou official recognition, after the constitution of 1987 enshrined freedom of religion</span></li></ol>
    </section>

